The last row of the ICE chart represents the equilibrium concentration of reactants and products. Still, as we do not know yet the extent of the reaction $x$ we can obtain numerical values of these concentrations. Still, we know that all these concentrations are not independent of each other, and they are linked through the equilibrium constant. By plugging the equilibrium concentrations in terms of $x$ in $K_c$, we can obtain a polynomial. The positive root of this polynomial--or the root that makes chemical sense--will lead to the numerical value for the extent of reaction. The order of the polynomials is related to the complexity and type of equilibrium. For example, acid-base equilibrium tends to be represented by second-order polynomials, whereas solubility equilibrium can be represented by third-degree polynomials. The use of polynomials to describe an equilibrium is not new, and this family of mathematical functions is well known in fields such as game theory, fluid mechanics, or statistical mechanics. You can use your graphing calculator to obtain the roots of any of the polynomials associated with a chemical equilibrium. Always remember only the positive root--or the root that makes chemical sense--has chemical meaning.
